---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Highlight from "../../components/ui/Highlight.astro";
import Link from "../../components/ui/Link.astro";

import { readme } from "../../config/site";
---

<DocsLayout
	breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Wi-Fi", path: "/wifi/" }]}
	title='Wi-Fi'
	description='Give Cheap Rex Display your Wi-Fi: over USB from your browser, or from a phone through the setup hotspot the board opens by itself.'>
	<h1 class='mb-4 text-3xl font-[650]'>Wi-Fi</h1>
	<p class='mb-8 text-lg'>
		The board needs your Wi-Fi once. It saves the network and joins it on every start. There are two ways to give it
		one, and both need a <strong>2.4 GHz</strong> network: the ESP32 doesn't see 5 GHz.
	</p>

	<ul class='mb-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6'>
		<li>
			<strong><Link href='/wifi/improv/'>Over USB, from your browser</Link>:</strong> right after installing, or any time
			later. Needs a computer and a browser with Web Serial.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong><Link href='#hotspot'>From a phone, through the setup hotspot</Link>:</strong> the board opens its own Wi-Fi
			when it has none to join. Works with any phone or computer.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2 id='order' class='mt-12 mb-3 text-xl font-[650]'>What it joins</h2>
	<p class='mb-4'>On every start, the board joins the first of these it has:</p>
	<ol class='list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6'>
		<li>the network saved on the board, through USB or the setup hotspot;</li>
		<li>
			the one in <code>secrets.h</code>, if you built the firmware yourself with it (see <Link
				offsite
				href={readme("wi-fi")}>Wi-Fi</Link
			> in the README);
		</li>
		<li>none: it opens the setup hotspot.</li>
	</ol>

	<h2 id='hotspot' class='mt-12 mb-3 text-xl font-[650]'>The setup hotspot</h2>
	<p class='mb-4'>
		With nothing to join, the board says so at the bottom of the boot animation, then opens its own Wi-Fi. The screen
		shows its name, <Highlight>crd-setup-</Highlight> and four letters, and a password that is new each time the hotspot opens.
	</p>
	<ol class='list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6'>
		<li>
			On a phone or computer, join that Wi-Fi with the password from the screen. If it isn't in the list yet, wait up to
			a minute or open the Wi-Fi settings again: some phones, iPhones especially, are slow to show a new network.
		</li>
		<li>A setup page opens by itself. If it doesn't, go to <code>http://192.168.4.1</code>.</li>
		<li>
			Pick your network, enter its password and choose <strong>Save</strong>. <strong>Scan again</strong> looks once more.
		</li>
	</ol>
	<p class='mt-4'>
		The board joins the network, saves it and restarts onto it, and the hotspot goes away. If it can't join, its screen
		says so and the hotspot stays: join it again and retry. Check the password, and that the network is 2.4 GHz.
	</p>
	<p class='mt-4'>
		Some phones leave a Wi-Fi without internet after a while. If yours does, tell it to stay on the hotspot.
	</p>

	<h2 id='moved' class='mt-12 mb-3 text-xl font-[650]'>Moving to another network</h2>
	<p>
		When the board can't join its network for 3 minutes, say because you took it somewhere else, it opens the setup
		hotspot by itself. While no device is on the hotspot, it keeps trying the old network, and goes back to it once it's
		there again. A power cut that restarts your router too won't send it to setup.
	</p>

	<h2 id='forget' class='mt-12 mb-3 text-xl font-[650]'>Forgetting the network</h2>
	<p class='mb-4'>
		Hold a finger on the boot animation. After a second the bottom line counts down, and after 5 seconds the board asks
		whether to forget the saved network. Tap <strong>Forget</strong> to forget it and restart, or <strong>Keep</strong>,
		or wait 10 seconds, to keep it. A short tap still skips the animation.
	</p>
	<p>
		Forgetting is only offered on the board itself, never over the network, and only when a network is saved on the
		board. Updating the firmware keeps the network; a new install or an erase wipes it.
	</p>
</DocsLayout>
